1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This is a truthful Windows Arm wheel demo. It is not a wheel launch.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The result is BLOCKED / NO-GO, and the value is a reusable way to turn
an appealing green signal into release-grade evidence.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The starting signal is real. Shapely already has a Windows ARM64 workflow lane.
A public job, “Build ARM64 wheels on windows-11-arm,” succeeded in run 32615526656 on 2026-08-23.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:40,000
But the matching release-windows-11-arm-ARM64 artifact, 12,940,021 bytes,
was expired on 2026-08-28. A successful historical job is not a reviewable wheel.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The current publication check is equally clear. Agent 6 queried official PyPI metadata
for Shapely version 2.1.2 and found zero win_arm64 files.

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
So there is no official file to inspect, install, or call shipped.

7
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Our workflow makes that boundary visible. The manager assigns the bounded question.
The design records immutable upstream pins, ownership, and release gates.

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The implementation review tests whether the proposed controls are actually executable.
The Windows Arm runbook gives maintainers the commands, stop conditions, and rollback path.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:30,000
That review found a decisive gap. The terminal specification invokes ci\wheel_evidence.py producer,
verify, and finalize, yet it does not contain the complete invoked helper.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This is finding HV-301-003. Until an immutable, reviewed helper is restored, compiled,
and negatively tested, the workflow is not implementation-ready.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:53,000
There is a second release stop. The current GEOS acquisition uses HTTP and has no SHA-256
verification before extraction. A production candidate needs HTTPS, authoritative digest provenance,

12
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
hash-before-extraction, verified cache markers, and positive and negative integrity tests.

13
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Why pursue this carefully? The demand numbers are opportunity proxies, not customer counts.
The workspace records 17,760 GitHub code-search hits for tool.cibuildwheel: code hits, not unique repositories.

14
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:25,000
It records 692 hits for win_arm64 with tool.cibuildwheel: a Windows Arm code-hit proxy,
not a dependency graph. And release 2025.7.7 of one curated downstream catalog lists 140 packages;

15
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:37,000
it is not total demand. The 12-finalist classification is also deliberately narrow:
1 project is blocked only by this foundation, 1 has it among agent-resolvable blockers,

16
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:49,000
6 retain external or vendor blockers, and 4 are not materially gated. These are planning categories,
not a forecast of users, downloads, performance, or power.

17
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:01,000
The path from NO-GO to release is exact. Restore and test the complete helper.
Obtain the authoritative GEOS HTTPS digest. On a physical windows-11-arm runner,

18
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:15,000
build and verify all 7 specified ABI rows. Retain the wheel, manifests, logs, and verifier records
for 30 days. Inspect tags, ARM64 PE binaries, repair output, licenses, clean installation,

19
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
upgrade, uninstall, isolated import, and the full Shapely test suite.

20
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Only then may Shapely’s package-owned release query official PyPI and match every expected
win_arm64 filename and SHA-256 digest. No product code was changed here.

21
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:45,000
No physical Arm test, binary inspection, performance improvement, power improvement, or wheel publication is claimed.
The demo outcome is a credible evidence path, with every missing gate made actionable.

